r=Report('01-business-succession','Business succession and aging owners','The opportunity is to make a business transferable. Owner age is only the beginning.','US small businesses; selected ownership models')
r.source('Census: business owners’ ages','https://www.census.gov/library/visualizations/2020/comm/business-owners-ages.html','September 2020; 2019 ABS, reference year 2018','Official historical owner demographics.','Owners are not firms or prospective sellers. This older snapshot is not a 2026 age estimate.')
r.source('SBA Advocacy: 2025 small-business profiles and technical notes','https://advocacy.sba.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/State_Profiles_2025_Technical-Notes.pdf','June 2025; underlying series have different reference years','Official statistical compilation.','Small-business totals include nonemployers. Counts do not measure transferable companies or businesses for sale.')
r.source('BizBuySell: 2025 year in review','https://www.bizbuysell.com/blog/2025-year-in-review/','January 30, 2026; calendar 2025','Marketplace transaction report.','Broker-reported closed transactions; selected coverage. Medians and average multiples are different statistics.')
r.source('BizBuySell: Q2 2026 Insight Report','https://www.bizbuysell.com/insight-report/','Q2 2026; page checked September 21','Marketplace transactions and sentiment.','A changing report URL. Reported transactions are not a national census; mix shifts can affect prices.')
r.source('Stanford GSB: 2026 search-fund study findings','https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/insights/search-funds-keep-offering-proven-path-ownership','July 13, 2026; study through December 2025','University summary of core search-fund research.','US and Canada; this institutional acquisition model differs from self-funded small-business buying. Primary summary reviewed; full study not relied upon.')
r.source('Axial: 2025 broken letters of intent','https://www.axial.net/forum/dead-deal-report-unpacking-2025s-broken-lois/','2026 report; unsuccessful 2025 transactions','Platform analysis of 75 failed transactions.','Selected failures with member explanations. No denominator for all attempted deals; cannot estimate the overall probability of failure.')
r.source('NCEO: employee ownership by the numbers','https://www.nceo.org/research/employee-ownership-by-the-numbers','Updated January 2026; 2023 Form 5500 data','Analysis of US retirement-plan filings.','Plans, companies, participants and new ownership transactions are different units. Filing and processing create a lag.')
r.source('Project Equity: Westbrae Nursery','https://project-equity.org/ownership-story/westbrae-nursery/','Undated account; April 2022 transition','Transaction adviser’s case account.','Six-employee business. Price, debt terms, post-transition profitability and an independent outcome evaluation are not disclosed.')
r.source('Project Equity: employee ownership for manufacturers','https://project-equity.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Employee_Ownership_for_Manufacturers_Project-Equity_91923.pdf','September 2023; Adams & Chittenden transition in 2019','Adviser case account, page 6.','Illustrates succession in a specialist manufacturer; no quantified causal effect on productivity or survival.')
r.source('SBA: 7(a) loans','https://www.sba.gov/loans/7a-loans/','Current program page checked September 21, 2026','Official program description.','The $5m maximum is a loan limit, not an acquisition-price limit or guaranteed approval. Eligibility and transaction terms require current lender review.')
r.source('Census: nonemployer statistics by demographics','https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/abs/data/nesd.html','Series page checked September 21, 2026','Official statistical scope and releases.','Nonemployer businesses are a separate population from employer-firm owner surveys. Neither population directly measures businesses for sale.')
r.section('brief','Executive brief','A large transition need. A selective acquisition market.',p('Aging owners create a reason for businesses to change hands. They do not establish how many attractive businesses are available, when owners will sell, or whether the next operator can preserve the earnings.', 'lead')+brief([
('Demography is a starting signal','The widely cited Census finding that more than half of owners were 55 or older describes 2018. It is historical evidence of an aging ownership base, not a current inventory of sellers.'+cite(1)),
('The transaction evidence is more restrained','BizBuySell recorded 9,586 sales in 2025, up 0.4%. Its Q2 2026 count fell 10% year over year. This does not show an accelerating wave of completed sales.'+cite(3,4)),
('The hard work is transferability','Financial verification, management succession and customer continuity determine whether an apparent asset can become a financeable, operating business. The diligence evidence points to concrete failure points.'+cite(6)),
('Several ownership routes already exist','External buyers, search funds and employee ownership solve different problems. An opening must improve an existing route or serve an uneconomic segment; age alone does not establish whitespace.')
])+note('<b>Reading guide.</b> Market observations and documented cases come first. Workflow designs, economics and possible businesses are analysis. The focus is ordinary US operating companies, not a forecast of retirement dates or a valuation recommendation.'))
r.section('evidence','Market evidence','Count sellers, businesses and transactions separately.',table('What the available numbers actually count',['Measure','Finding','Interpretation'],[
('All small businesses', '36.2m businesses; 62.3m employees in the SBA’s 2025 profile.'+cite(2),'Includes businesses with no employees. This population cannot be multiplied by a retirement rate to produce an acquisition market.'),
('Age of owners','More than half aged 55+ in the Census’s 2018 reference-year data.'+cite(1),'An owner can have several businesses; a firm can have several owners. Age does not reveal readiness, health, succession plans or sale intention.'),
('Reported closed sales, 2025','9,586; median sale price $350,000; average cash-flow multiple 2.61×.'+cite(3),'A useful view of brokered Main Street transactions. Neither comprehensive coverage nor an EBITDA valuation benchmark for larger companies.'),
('Reported closed sales, Q2 2026','2,117; median sale price $349,250; average cash-flow multiple 2.7×.'+cite(4),'Volume down 10% year over year while the average multiple rose 2%. Falling deal count does not imply cheap surviving deals.'),
('Core search-fund purchases','Median purchase price $16m for 2024–25 acquisitions; about 20 months to acquire.'+cite(5),'A distinct segment. Institutional search results should not be applied to a $350,000 owner-operated business.')
])+p('Nonemployers are measured separately by Census. A consulting practice, rental entity or part-time sole proprietorship can be an economic activity without being a going concern a buyer can operate after the owner leaves. Employer status is a better first filter, but still says nothing about profit quality, manager depth or willingness to sell.'+cite(11))+r.chart('Where selected transactions broke after signing an LOI',[
('Non-earnings diligence',25.3,'25.3%'),('Earnings discrepancies',21.3,'21.3%'),('Financing',10.7,'10.7%')],30,'Percent of 75 unsuccessful transactions','Axial, 2025. Selected categories, not an exhaustive breakdown. The first two categories sum to 46.6%. This measures reasons among failed deals, not a 46.6% failure rate across deals.',[6])+p('The better-supported thesis is an enduring coordination problem: some owners need a path out, while buyers need credible earnings and a business that survives the handoff. The pace depends on individual readiness, financing and asset quality. A demographic story does not resolve any of these filters.'))
r.section('cases','Documented cases','A handoff changes management as well as ownership.',case('Employee ownership / Retail / April 2022','Westbrae Nursery: build the successor inside the company', [('6','employees in the adviser’s case'),('2020','owner raised retirement and employee ownership'),('2022','cooperative transition completed')], 'The Berkeley nursery’s owner began discussing retirement in 2020 and contacted Project Equity that fall. The April 2022 transfer followed financial feasibility work, legal structuring, governance design and employee management training. Continued support covered financial indicators, board work and management evaluation.'+cite(8), 'The buyer was already connected to customers, products and daily work. The missing capability was running and governing the business. A useful service would have to connect financial readiness with management development, rather than merely publish a listing.', 'This is the adviser’s account. It establishes a completed transition and the work involved; it does not disclose purchase economics or prove a profitability benefit.')+case('Employee ownership / Specialist manufacturing / 2019','Adams & Chittenden: preserving a narrow technical capability', [('2019','transfer to a worker cooperative'),('30 years','operating history described in the 2023 account'),('Not disclosed','sale price and financing terms')], 'The Berkeley scientific-glass manufacturer served a specialized global customer base. Its founders struggled to identify a plausible external buyer, then transferred the company to employees with Project Equity’s assistance. The 2023 account describes continued operation through the pandemic.'+cite(9), 'A niche can be valuable yet difficult to match with an outside operator. Keeping skilled employees and customer-specific production knowledge together may preserve more value than selling equipment or introducing an inexperienced buyer.', 'Continuation is observed in an adviser narrative. There is no counterfactual showing the business would otherwise have closed, or measured improvement in output or margins.')+case('Acquisition channel / US and Canada / 2026 study','Search funds: money and motivated buyers still need time', [('~50%','acquisition rate for funds launched 2021–24'),('58%','historical aggregate acquisition rate'),('$16m','median acquisition price, 2024–25')], 'Stanford’s 2026 summary describes a model in which entrepreneurs raise search capital, locate a company and become its operator. Even in this organized channel, roughly half of the recent launch cohorts acquired a business. Its typical search duration is around 20 months.'+cite(5), 'The friction is more substantial than locating a name and email address. Deal selection, negotiation, financing and operator fit still matter. Screening software is useful only if it improves decisions or releases expensive search time.', 'The cohorts and price statistic cover different periods. These findings do not measure owner retirement or the success rate of self-funded buyers. Aggregate investment returns are deliberately not treated as typical buyer outcomes.'))
r.section('workflows','The transfer process','Make dependence visible before promising independence.',p('For the brainstorms’ seller-preparation and operating-intelligence ideas, the valuable object is a verified account of how the business earns money. A folder of documents is an input. A successor who can price jobs, keep customers and manage exceptions is the outcome. This is a proposed workflow, not a documented AI deployment.')+flow('From owner knowledge to a tested handoff',[
('Trace the earnings','Reconcile the ledger, tax returns, bank deposits and job-level records. Keep adjustments tied to evidence.'),('Locate dependence','Record who prices, approves credit, resolves complaints and controls each important relationship.'),('Practice delegation','Have a successor handle real cases. Compare decisions and investigate exceptions before the owner exits.'),('Transfer and maintain','Change authorities and contacts; assign owners to procedures; preserve a route for unresolved cases.')
],'AI may organize interviews, reconcile records and retrieve precedents. The people involved must verify facts and accept the transfer of responsibility.')+table('What needs to survive the owner’s departure',['Business function','Evidence to collect','A useful acceptance test'],[
('Revenue','Customer concentration, renewal dates, relationship owners, contracts and pricing exceptions.','The successor conducts a renewal or quote without an undisclosed owner intervention.'),('Production or service delivery','Job histories, failed jobs, diagnostic steps, supplier substitutions and quality checks.','A representative difficult job is completed at the expected margin and quality.'),('Financial control','Cash reconciliation, receivables aging, recurring expenses, owner adjustments and capital needs.','An independent reviewer can reproduce normalized earnings and explain the cash conversion.'),('Management','Approval rights, scheduling, licenses, key employee responsibilities and incentives.','The company operates through an agreed absence period with tracked escalations, not just an optimistic interview.')
])+p('The first commercial constraint may be trust. An owner must expose sensitive financial and customer information before knowing whether a transaction will close. Advisers already embedded in the business can reduce that friction. A new provider still needs explicit permission, a narrow initial deliverable and a reason for the owner to keep the records current. These are distribution and delivery hypotheses, not measured customer demand.'))
r.section('economics','Economics','The price of the business is only one cost.',p('A small acquisition often buys both an income stream and a job. If the buyer plans to hire a manager, the seller’s owner earnings must support that replacement cost. Comparing a multiple of owner cash flow with a multiple of manager-run EBITDA can create a false bargain.')+table('Illustration: an attractive headline can leave little residual cash',['Annual item','Amount','Assumption'],[
('Advertised owner cash flow','$300,000','Starting assumption; not a market median.'),('Replacement general manager','−$140,000','Fully loaded compensation, assumed.'),('Maintenance capital spending','−$30,000','Recurring replacement spending, assumed.'),('Annual debt service','−$100,000','Assumed total interest and principal; no financing quote.'),('Residual before tax and working-capital changes','$30,000','$300,000 − $140,000 − $30,000 − $100,000.')
])+p('The result changes if the buyer performs the manager’s job, but then part of the return compensates the buyer’s labor. A working-capital shortfall, lost customer or delayed handoff can consume the residual. AI savings should enter an acquisition case only after their implementation cost, timing and cash impact have been tested. A possible improvement should not be counted as already purchased earnings.')+p('SBA’s 7(a) program allows financing for eligible ownership changes and lists a $5m maximum loan. That makes it relevant to some small acquisitions, but the limit is not a promise of credit or a maximum purchase price. The repayment case still depends on the actual company and transaction.'+cite(10))+p('A service provider faces different economics. On the illustrative $350,000-sized transaction, a hypothetical $20,000 preparation engagement equals 5.7% of sale value. That fee needs a clear benefit and a willing payer. Larger firms can support more professional work; smaller firms may need standardized delivery or an established adviser channel. This arithmetic explains why a broad market count is insufficient.'))
r.section('segments','Segments and alternatives','Different successors need different infrastructure.',table('A segmented market, not a single “silver tsunami”',['Segment','What is scarce','Likely route and constraint'],[
('Owner-operated local business','A buyer willing to do the job and finance the purchase.','Individual acquisition; low deal value limits advisory fees.'),('Specialist employer business','Technical continuity, licenses and trusted customer relationships.','Management buyout, employee ownership or a qualified trade buyer; successor training can dominate the timetable.'),('Manager-run lower-middle-market firm','Credible earnings, growth and a strong operating team.','Search fund, independent sponsor or PE buyer; attractive assets face competition.'),('Family business','Agreement over control, economics and leadership.','Family or mixed succession; software cannot resolve conflicting preferences.'),('Weak or nontransferable enterprise','Sustainable profit after replacing the owner.','Restructuring, asset sale or closure may be economically rational; a matching platform cannot create missing earnings.')
])+p('Employee ownership is an established route, not an empty category. NCEO’s January 2026 analysis identifies 6,609 ESOP plans at 6,411 companies using 2023 filings, including 309 new plans that year. ESOPs also differ from the worker cooperatives in the cases above; the legal, financing and administrative demands should not be conflated.'+cite(7))+p('Counterevidence matters. Some owners have capable family successors. Some prefer to keep a profitable business indefinitely. Others cannot sell at a price that meets their retirement expectations. Buyers may rationally reject a company whose earnings disappear when the owner leaves. Each possibility reduces the portion of demographic need that becomes a paid transaction.'))
r.section('implications','Opportunity hypotheses','Sell a verifiable improvement in the handoff.',p('The research supports testing the needs below. It does not establish their market size, willingness to pay or defensibility. They connect to the seller-preparation, diligence and knowledge-transfer ideas in the <a href="../research/analyses/brainstorm-synthesis.md">brainstorm synthesis</a>.')+table('Possible opportunities and who captures the value',['Need / likely buyer','Why a gap might persist','Possible business and failure condition'],[
('Transfer-ready operating records / owner or adviser','Books and procedures may be adequate for the current owner but inadequate for an outside operator.','A preparation service with reusable software. Weak if accountants and brokers can supply the same evidence cheaply.'),('Knowledge transfer / acquiring operator','Critical exceptions and relationships are difficult to infer from a data room.','A transition service tied to successor performance. Weak if capture remains bespoke interviewing with little reuse.'),('Evidence-backed screening / buyer or lender','A large pipeline may contain few viable, financeable businesses.','Sector-specific screening and verified operating data. Weak if outputs do not change acceptance, pricing or diligence time.'),('Acquire and improve / permanent owner','The operator could retain the value of better processes.','An acquisition strategy rather than a software company. Weak if purchase price already capitalizes the gains or improvements depend on the departing owner.')
])+p('Existing brokers, accountants, lenders and experienced operators are potential beneficiaries because they already control part of the transaction. Undifferentiated listing or summarization tools face a harder test: they need to improve completion or decision quality without adding another costly step. A compelling starting point is a recurring diligence failure in one sector, with a buyer who can identify what correcting it is worth.'))
r.section('questions','What would change the view','Find the economically transferable subset.',table('Next evidence, before choosing a company',['Question','Useful evidence','What would weaken the thesis'],[
('Are owners ready to act?','A defined local or sector cohort: intent, timetable, successor plans and actual preparation spending.','Interest remains abstract; no willingness to share data or pay.'),('What prevents a viable transfer?','Review completed and failed deals with advisers; distinguish no buyer, no financing and untransferable operations.','The main constraint is valuation disagreement rather than missing information.'),('Can preparation change a result?','Matched before/after measures of document requests, adjusted earnings disputes, completion time and buyer confidence.','More documentation adds work without improving a decision or handoff.'),('Can delivery repeat?','Several paid engagements in one segment, including labor hours, exceptions, update effort and referral conversion.','Each company requires a different specialist team; margin disappears.'),('Does the transition hold?','Customer retention, key-staff retention, owner escalations and cash performance after transfer.','A clean data room masks persistent dependence on the former owner.')
])+p('Timing is likely to be measured in individual transitions, not a single national wave. A useful update would track an identifiable sector’s seller intentions through to listing, diligence, financing and post-transfer operation. That would connect the demographic thesis to an actual commercial process.'))
r.evidence.append(dict(title='Succession cash-flow illustration',kind='illustrative',unit='USD per year',assumptions=dict(owner_cash_flow=300000,replacement_manager=140000,maintenance_capex=30000,debt_service=100000),result=30000,formula='300000 - 140000 - 30000 - 100000; before tax and working-capital changes',source_ids=[]))
